   (d) TestHandleResolve_DecisionsFileUnreadableReportsHonestMessage
   corrupts transfer_decisions.json and asserts the load-failure message,
   and separately checks it does NOT contain "already confirmed/rejected"
   or "not a known pair" — genuinely exercises describeAlreadyResolved's
   first branch, not just its outcome. The doc comment enumerates all
   four cases accurately.
   (e) comment/code match confirmed by inspection.

8. FULL SUITE. `go test -count=1 ./...` green across the whole module in
   the actual repo tree (read-only), node present.

9. `.swarm/tier3/S5/accept.sh` (rewritten oracle) run standalone against a
   fresh rsync of the submitted tree: all 11 checks PASS, ORACLE: PASS.
   It behaviourally tests the fake-verdict case (injects a harness that
   reports FAIL while exiting 0) rather than grepping for a token, and
   restores the harness via a trap even on interruption — a real
   improvement over the attempt-4 oracle that shared the Makefile-scanning
   blind spot. The rewrite legitimately drops the Makefile-idiom checks
   (GOCMD alias / define / line-continuation enumeration): those attack
   vectors are moot under the new design (there is nothing Make-side left
   to evade), so this is not a coverage loss, it is dead-code removal
   matching the redesign. I did not find a case the new oracle should
   test and doesn't; the closest gap is that it does not exercise the
   test-cache-replay angle explicitly, but I independently confirmed above
   that Go's cache cannot replay a stale PASS here regardless.

10. PROCESS DEVIATION — single worker instead of blind N-version at
    Tier 3, as declared in REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md. The lead's stated
    rationale is that N-version "exists to explore a design space" and
    the user's directive removed that ambiguity. That rationale is in
    tension with this project's own constitution (CLAUDE.md), which
    states the Tier-3 N-version pair here "differ[s] by model tier only,
    so N-version ... catches slips and misreadings rather than shared
    blind spots" — i.e. its stated purpose at this project is NOT
    exploring a design space, it is catching a single implementer's
    mechanical errors, which remains a risk even when the design itself
    is no longer in question (an off-by-one in the opt-out check, a
    missed call site, a stale comment are all "slips," not design
    choices). So the declared justification for skipping the blind pair
    does not actually match the constitution's own reason for having it,
    and I judge the deviation as under-justified — a shortcut taken after
    a long, three-times-failed task, dressed in reasoning that doesn't
    hold up against the document that authorizes N-version in the first
    place. That said, this is a process finding, not a defect finding:
    every angle I attacked against the actual attempt-5 artifact (the
    "slip" class N-version exists to catch) held up under adversarial
    testing above, so I am not withholding PASS on the work product
    itself over it. The lead should treat this critique as a live
    process gap to correct, not as retroactively invalidating attempt 5.

No FAIL condition found in the implementation itself. Recommending PASS
on the artifact, with the Tier-3 process deviation (item 10) flagged for
